# Phase 2 — OmicsFlow Internal R Package Architecture

Convert the usability/orchestration layer in `R/` into a proper installable R package while preserving the validated scientific module architecture.

## Design Decisions

### Packaging strategy: in-place overlay

Rather than creating a separate subdirectory (`OmicsFlow/`), we packageize the repository root itself. This is the standard pattern for GitHub-installable R packages (e.g. `remotes::install_github("user/repo")`). The root already contains the `R/` directory with all usability functions.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The scientific modules (`modules/`, `reports/`, `data/`, etc.) are **not** part of the R package source. They remain as repository-level assets referenced by the orchestration layer at runtime. This keeps the package lightweight and avoids bundling large analysis scripts or data.

### What the package contains vs. what it orchestrates

| Layer | In the R package | Accessed at runtime |
|-------|-----------------|-------------------|
| User API | `omicsflow()`, `validate_inputs()`, `generate_metadata_templates()` | — |
| Internal helpers | `detect_sample_ids()`, `infer_patient_ids()`, etc. | — |
| Console formatters | `msg_ok()`, `msg_warn()`, etc. | — |
| Scientific modules | — | `modules/rna/`, `modules/methylation/`, etc. via `system()` |
| Quarto reports | — | `reports/OmicsFlow_Report.qmd` via `quarto render` |
| Metadata schema | — | `modules/utils_metadata.R` via `source()` |

